**Proof.** The ultrametric inequality is fine. “Equality unless two vertices” is the unique-min vs tie split; on this polygon a hypotenuse tie is exactly the two outer vertices (`gcd(n₁,m₁)=1`). The Weierstrass support in weights `(n,m)=(3g,2g)` has unique max weight `6g` on `(2,0)` and `(0,3)`, every extra monomial strictly below. The jump from that `(n,m)`-polygon to an *arbitrary* divisorial `ν` is the missing step: the `(n,m)` face does not by itself control every `L(i,j)=iν(F)+jν(G)`. It fills from this polytope (third-quadrant dual cone = unique outer edge; mixed signs unique at one outer vertex). Dual-cone scans: 0 covering failures.

A tie is case (b). Exclusivity fails when the two principal initials do not cancel, so (b) and (c) both hold. Exhibit: `Φ=v³−u²`, `F=x³`, `G=y²`, `Q=y⁶−x⁶`, `ν=−deg`. The useful theorem is covering OR: off the ray, a negative order forces (c).

